{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And Jacob came whole from Paddan-aram.</b> It is like saying to a friend: So-and-so went out from between the teeth of lions and came whole — so too here, 'And Jacob came whole' from Laban and from Esau. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 33:18). But Rabbi Abraham explained that he came in peace and no mishap befell him at all, for now the Scripture begins to relate the incident of Dinah. In my opinion, however, the verse says this because in Sukkot he was still in fear of Esau, for Sukkot is across the Jordan, eastward, in the kingdom of Sihon — if it is the one mentioned in the book of Joshua (Joshua 13:27) — and even if it is elsewhere, behold it is closer to Seir; and until he was in the land of Canaan his heart was not at rest, for then he knew that Esau would not touch him, because his father was nearby from there, or because the people of the land would help him since his father was a prince of Elohim among them, or because the merit of the land would deliver him. Therefore it now says that he came whole to the land of his father's sojournings, for Elohim had delivered him on the way from the hand of all his enemies. And they said in Bereshit Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 78:16): All those months that our father Jacob spent in Sukkot, he was paying tribute to Esau with that gift, for he was afraid of him there, and would send him that gift every month, or once a year:" "<b>And he encamped facing the city.</b> He did not want to be a guest in the city, but wanted the beginning of his entry into the land to be on his own property. Therefore he camped in the field and purchased the place, and this was to take hold in the land. And this act was a sign, for that place would be conquered before him first, before the inhabitants of the land would be dispossessed on account of his seed, as I explained regarding Abraham (Ramban on Genesis 12:6). And our Rabbis said (Bereshit Rabbah 79:6): He entered just before sunset and established for himself boundary-lines. According to their opinion, this was not his intention, but in any event the occurrence alluded to the future, as we have said. But Rabbi Abraham said (Ibn Ezra on Genesis 33:19) that Scripture mentioned this to inform us that there is great virtue to the land of Israel, and whoever has a portion in it is accounted as though he has a portion in the World to Come:"], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {0 "The word שָׁלֵם (whole/complete) is read adverbially per Rashi — Jacob arrived intact in body and possessions. The alternative reading from 'Rabbi Abraham' (Ibn Ezra) takes it as 'in peace, without mishap.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:39:19.830363086Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}